lessons.notes:
## Basics

```cpp
#include <string>
std::string a = "Hello";
std::string b{"World"};
std::string c = a + ", " + b + "!";
c += '\n';
```

## Common members

| Member | Does |
|--------|------|
| `size()` / `length()` | number of chars |
| `empty()` | true if "" |
| `s[i]`, `s.at(i)` | char access |
| `substr(pos, len)` | piece of the string |
| `find(text)` | position or `std::string::npos` |
| `replace(pos, len, text)` | replace a range |
| `push_back(c)`, `pop_back()` | add/remove at the end |
| `insert`, `erase` | edit in the middle |

## Conversions

```cpp
int n = std::stoi("42");
double d = std::stod("3.5");
std::string s = std::to_string(99);
```
`std::stoi` throws an exception for invalid text.

## Reading lines

```cpp
std::string line;
while (std::getline(std::cin, line)) {
    // process each line
}
```

## Character helpers (`<cctype>`)

`std::isalpha`, `std::isdigit`, `std::isspace`, `std::toupper`, `std::tolower`. Cast the `char` to `unsigned char` before passing it.

## std::string_view (C++17)

`std::string_view` is a lightweight, read-only view of text: great for function parameters that only read strings without copying.

## Title case idea

Keep a flag "next letter starts a word". Set it after a space; when it is set, make the letter uppercase, otherwise lowercase.

lessons.example:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <cctype>

int main() {
    std::string s{"The quick brown fox"};
    std::cout << "length: " << s.size() << '\n';
    auto pos = s.find("brown");
    std::cout << "brown at " << pos << ", next 5 chars: " << s.substr(pos, 5) << '\n';
    int vowels = 0;
    for (char c : s) {
        char l = static_cast<char>(std::tolower(static_cast<unsigned char>(c)));
        if (std::string("aeiou").find(l) != std::string::npos) ++vowels;
    }
    std::cout << "vowels: " << vowels << '\n';
    std::cout << s + " jumps" << '\n';
}

