lessons.notes:
## Containers at a glance

| Container | Use it for | Lookup |
|-----------|-----------|--------|
| `vector<T>` | ordered list | by index O(1) |
| `map<K,V>` | sorted dictionary | O(log n) |
| `unordered_map<K,V>` | fast dictionary | O(1) average |
| `set<T>` | sorted unique values | O(log n) |
| `unordered_set<T>` | fast unique values | O(1) average |
| `stack<T>` / `queue<T>` | LIFO / FIFO | top/front |
| `priority_queue<T>` | always get the largest | O(log n) |

## map essentials

```cpp
std::map<std::string, int> m;
m["apple"] += 3;              // creates "apple" with 0, then adds 3
m.insert({"pear", 2});
if (auto it = m.find("kiwi"); it != m.end()) {
    std::cout << it->second;
}
m.erase("pear");
```

Careful: `m[key]` **inserts** missing keys. Use `find`, `count` or `contains` (C++20) just to check.

## pair and structured bindings

```cpp
std::pair<std::string, int> p{"Ana", 20};
auto [name, years] = p;     // C++17
```

## Iteration order

`map` and `set` iterate in ascending key order. `unordered_*` order is unspecified and may change between runs or compilers - never rely on it for output.

## Word counting pattern

```cpp
std::map<std::string, int> freq;
std::string w;
while (std::cin >> w) ++freq[w];
for (const auto& [word, n] : freq) std::cout << word << ' ' << n << '\n';
```

