## Iterators and ranges

Algorithms take a half-open range `[first, last)`: `v.begin()` points to the first element, `v.end()` one past the last.

## Common algorithms

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <numeric>

std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
auto it = std::find(v.begin(), v.end(), 42);
if (it != v.end()) { /* found */ }
int total = std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0);
auto mx = *std::max_element(v.begin(), v.end());
std::reverse(v.begin(), v.end());
int n = std::count(v.begin(), v.end(), 7);
```

## Lambdas

```cpp
[capture](parameters) { body }
```

- `[]` captures nothing.
- `[x]` copies `x`; `[&x]` refers to `x`.
- `[&]` captures everything by reference, `[=]` by copy.

```cpp
int threshold = 10;
auto above = std::count_if(v.begin(), v.end(),
                           [threshold](int x) { return x > threshold; });
```

## Comparators

A comparator returns `true` if `a` must come **before** `b`. It must be a *strict* ordering: never return true for equal elements (use `<`, not `<=`).

Use `std::stable_sort` when equal elements must keep their original order.

## C++20 ranges

Modern compilers support `std::ranges::sort(v);` - no need for `begin()`/`end()`. The classic form works everywhere, so this course uses it.

lessons.example:
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <numeric>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v{7, 2, 9, 4, 4, 1};
    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
    for (int x : v) std::cout << x << ' ';
    std::cout << '\n';
    std::cout << "sum = " << std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0) << '\n';
    std::cout << "max = " << *std::max_element(v.begin(), v.end()) << '\n';
    int big = std::count_if(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { return x > 3; });
    std::cout << "greater than 3: " << big << '\n';
    std::transform(v.begin(), v.end(), v.begin(), [](int x) { return x * x; });
    for (int x : v) std::cout << x << ' ';
    std::cout << '\n';
}

