lessons.notes:
## Defining a class

```cpp
class BankAccount {
public:
    explicit BankAccount(long long initial = 0) : balance_(initial) {}

    void deposit(long long amount) { balance_ += amount; }

    bool withdraw(long long amount) {
        if (amount > balance_) return false;   // protect the invariant
        balance_ -= amount;
        return true;
    }

    long long balance() const { return balance_; }

private:
    long long balance_;
};
```

## Key ideas

- **Members** - the data (`balance_`). A trailing `_` is a common naming style.
- **Member functions** - behavior (`deposit`).
- **Access**: `public` (anyone), `private` (only the class itself).
- **Constructor** - same name as the class, no return type. Use the initializer list `: balance_(initial)`.
- **`explicit`** prevents surprising automatic conversions from a single argument.
- **`const` member function** - promises not to modify the object.

## Using objects

```cpp
BankAccount acc{100};
acc.deposit(50);
if (!acc.withdraw(500)) std::cout << "insufficient funds\n";
std::cout << acc.balance() << '\n';   // 150
```

## Destructors

`~ClassName()` runs automatically when an object is destroyed. It is where resources are released - the basis of **RAII** (covered later).

## struct

```cpp
