## Function templates

```cpp
template <typename T>
T clamp_value(T x, T lo, T hi) {
    return x < lo ? lo : (hi < x ? hi : x);
}
```

The compiler **instantiates** a separate version for each type you use: `clamp_value<int>`, `clamp_value<double>`, ... Usually it **deduces** `T` from the arguments, but you can be explicit: `clamp_value<double>(5, 0, 1)`.

## Requirements

A template only compiles for types that support the operations it uses. `largest` above needs `<` and copying. Calling it with a type lacking `<` fails at compile time.

## Class templates

```cpp
template <typename T>
class Stack {
public:
    void push(const T& x) { data_.push_back(x); }
    T pop() { T x = data_.back(); data_.pop_back(); return x; }
    bool empty() const { return data_.empty(); }
private:
    std::vector<T> data_;
};

Stack<std::string> s;
```

## Multiple parameters

```cpp
template <typename K, typename V>
struct Entry { K key; V value; };
```

## Concepts (C++20)

```cpp
#include <concepts>
template <std::integral T>
T half(T x) { return x / 2; }
```

## Where templates live

Template code is normally written entirely in header files, because the compiler needs the full definition at each use.

## Templates everywhere

`std::vector<T>`, `std::map<K, V>`, `std::sort`, `std::unique_ptr<T>` - the whole standard library is generic code.

