## RAII in one example

```cpp
class Timer {
public:
    Timer() : start_(std::chrono::steady_clock::now()) {}
    ~Timer() { /* print elapsed time */ }
private:
    std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point start_;
};
```
Every standard container, stream, smart pointer and lock (`std::lock_guard`) uses RAII.

## Smart pointers (`<memory>`)

```cpp
auto a = std::make_unique<Widget>(args);   // sole owner
auto b = std::move(a);                     // ownership moves, a is null
auto s1 = std::make_shared<Widget>();      // shared
auto s2 = s1;                              // count = 2
```
Rules: use `make_unique` / `make_shared`, never `new`. Pass raw pointers or references to functions that only *use* an object.

## std::optional (C++17)

```cpp
std::optional<int> find_index(const std::vector<int>& v, int x) {
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i)
        if (v[i] == x) return static_cast<int>(i);
    return std::nullopt;
}
```

## Safe number parsing with from_chars

```cpp
#include <charconv>
std::optional<int> parse_int(const std::string& s) {
    int value{};
    auto [ptr, ec] = std::from_chars(s.data(), s.data() + s.size(), value);
    if (ec != std::errc{} || ptr != s.data() + s.size()) return std::nullopt;
    return value;
}
```
No exceptions, no locale surprises, detects trailing garbage and overflow.

